"\nTARGET 1 AGENTS.md prose\u002dentrypoint\nIDENTITY d41c545600fcd3f38584e585f25a2b75d688d0c3 c9cd148fbf052228d237a02e751e65b4d4edc2f39c75712b126650bc18715489 RANGES [[1, 356]] MASKED [48, 209]\n181:   `upstream/next`, `git checkout next \u002d\u002d <the feature paths>`, one commit. The old\n182:   `git rebase \u002d\u002donto upstream/next next pr/<name>` recipe is retired \u2014 it silently\n183:   produces an empty branch once the feature has been merged into `next` (D\u002dWORKFLOW\u002d006).\n184:   `.githooks/pre\u002dpush` guards `pr/*`; install it with an **absolute** path\n185:   (`git config core.hooksPath \"$(git rev\u002dparse \u002d\u002dshow\u002dtoplevel)/.githooks\"`).\n186: \u002d Remove a worktree with `tools/fork\u002dworktree remove`, never `git worktree remove \u002d\u002dforce`\n187:   (D\u002dWORKFLOW\u002d005): it cannot tell a checkout from hours of build output.\n188: \u002d Issues go on the fork: always `gh \u002d\u002drepo pixelelated/distribution` (`gh` defaults to upstream\n189:   here, which has Issues disabled).\n190: \u002d The milestone body is the current ordered plan: current/next work, dependencies\n191:   and exit evidence. Keep open titles aligned as `M7.P1: ...`; M comes from the\n192:   milestone name, P from its body. Update both when priorities change; preserve\n193:   closed titles (`milestone\u002dphase\u002dnaming.md`, D\u002dWORKFLOW\u002d139).\n194: \u002d User\u002dfacing behavior changes need a follow\u002dup docs PR to the separate\n195:   `ROCKNIX/rocknix.org` repo \u2014 code and the public docs must not drift.\n196: \u002d Every package the fork introduces is at its latest upstream release or pinned with a\n197:   stated reason: `tools/fork\u002dpackage\u002dfreshness` exits 0 before a `pr/*` branch is cut.\n198: \n199: ## The rules that bind every session\n200: \n201: These are the maintainer's calls, each recorded in `docs/decision\u002dregister.md` by ID.\n202: They are not style; each one exists because its absence cost somebody an evening.\n203: \n204: \u002d **A handheld is a person's device and its cloud is their data.** Nothing runs on one\n205:   without a per\u002daction yes: not a reboot, not a game launch, not injected input, not a\n206:   screenshot, not a sync, upload or deletion in their cloud, and not the copy of an update\n207:   into `~/.update`. A general offer of device testing is not a standing yes; each action is\n208:   asked for by name with what it writes, sends and leaves behind. Reads need no question\n209: [MASKED sensitive\u002dlabelled line; not read unredacted]\n210:   command runs through `tools/device\u002dact` (D\u002dQA\u002d011, D\u002dQA\u002d015, D\u002dQA\u002d021).\n211: \u002d **Can this be done on the VM?** Asked and answered in writing, in the issue, before every\n212:   test, proof or measurement. Only a named physical fact (a radio, a battery, a board's\n213:   boot, a real panel, a tailnet) moves it off the VM; \"a real provider\" and \"the device\n214:   already has the history\" are not a no (D\u002dQA\u002d007, D\u002dQA\u002d033).\n215: \u002d **Acceptance criteria are agent\u002dfirst.** Every checkbox is written so an agent can verify\n216:   it and names the artifact that ticks it \u2014 a frame, a suite's PASS line, a stamp, a journal\n217:   line. A person's testing and feedback are the source of issues, never the check that\n218:   closes one; a checkbox that says \"the maintainer's word\" without a physical fact is not a\n219:   criterion (D\u002dQA\u002d044). Say *checkbox* or *open item to verify*, never a bare *box*, which\n220:   here means a machine (D\u002dQA\u002d045).\n221: \u002d **Every out\u002dof\u002dband request becomes a fork issue the same session**, quoting the\n222:   maintainer's words verbatim, with what exists today and acceptance criteria (D\u002dQA\u002d012).\n223: \u002d **Decisions go in `docs/decision\u002dregister.md` the same session**, cited by ID and never\n224:   re\u002dargued; the table is append\u002donly. Run `tools/archaeology <terms>` before calling\n225:   anything pending, open or new \u2014 the record has settled more than any session remembers.\n226: \u002d **A learning becomes a rule, a tool or a work\u002dlog entry, never only a memory.** Append a\n227:   timestamped entry to `docs/work\u002dlogs/<yyyy_mm>\u002dwork_logs/<yyyy_mm_dd>\u002dwork_log.md`, then\n228:   `tools/work\u002dlog\u002dindex \u002d\u002dwrite`. A procedure becomes a tool or a flag, not prose.\n229: \u002d **Verify the artifact, not the report.** A claim about behaviour cites a file's bytes, a\n230:   row's timestamp, a process, a frame. A name is not a behaviour; a summary is not the\n231:   source; a guard that cannot run has not passed; a promise is not a mechanism \u2014 name what\n232:   is watching a long job or say nothing is.\n233: \u002d **Every change lands on devices that already have state.** Check the upgrade path (a\n234:   device keeping its `/storage`) and a clean install before anything is published;\n235:   `tools/vm\u002dupgrade\u002drehearsal` runs the upgrade half on the VM.\n236: \u002d **Never edit a shell tool while a run of it is in flight**, and never `pkill \u002df` a\n237:   pattern the shell's own argv carries; long\u002dlived processes get a pidfile.\n238: \u002d **Keep each ES change with its flow and screenshot evidence.** Update the\n239:   canonical menu/IA/flow reference and accompanying reviewed happy\u002dpath and\n240:   branch frames for navigation, behavior or screen\u002dcontent changes, including\n241:   copy\u002donly edits. A nonvisual change records the exercised flow and unchanged\n242:   presentation. Source, references and the exact\u002dsource frame index precede\n243:   completion or pin promotion (`es\u002dui\u002dstyle\u002dguide.md`, D\u002dWORKFLOW\u002d155/156).\n244: \u002d **Clarity, then brevity, then sized to the space** for every string a player reads, and\n245:   surprise them as little as possible; time to play (interface \u2192 first frame, exit \u2192 next\n246:   first frame) is measured on every image (D\u002dUI\u002d045, D\u002dUI\u002d042, D\u002dCLOUD\u002d098).\n247: \n248: ## The rule files, and when each loads\n249: \n250: Generated from the rule files' front matter by `tools/rules\u002dcheck \u002d\u002dwrite\u002dagents`; the\n251: default run fails when this block is stale. \"Every session\" means the file has no\n252: `paths:` glob or a `**` glob; Codex does not load any of them on its own, so open the\n253: every\u002dsession ones at the start of a session and a scoped one before touching its paths.\n254: \n255: <!\u002d\u002d rules\u002dindex:begin (generated by tools/rules\u002dcheck \u002d\u002dwrite\u002dagents; do not edit by hand) \u002d\u002d>\n256: **Every session** (no `paths:` glob, or `**`):\n257: \n258: \u002d `adversarial\u002dcouncil.md` \u002d\u002d Adversarial\u002dreview routing \u2014 code audits select a local, cross\u002dlab two\u002dmodel or extended three\u002dmodel review; a formal council remains five verified seats. All external reviewers use the Facilitator. Read before an audit, challenge pass or council deliberation.\n259: \u002d `bugs\u002dare\u002dagent\u002dfirst.md` \u002d\u002d What a bug is here: fixed to the best of our ability means fixed and closed; every criterion is verified on the VM by an agent; what the VM cannot verify is not a known bug but an item the community tests or a thing to keep an eye on; no known bug is open when a build is called a release candidate (D\u002dQA\u002d051).\n260: \u002d `ceremonies.md` \u002d\u002d Which ceremony is owed and when \u002d\u002d a friction entry's issue, a mini\u002dretro, the weekly and monthly summaries, the work\u002dlog index, the register lint, a blindspot's guard, a code audit, a futro \u002d\u002d as a state machine tools/ceremony\u002dcheck reads from the record; what a missing one refuses (D\u002dWORKFLOW\u002d028, D\u002dQA\u002d040).\n261: \u002d `decision\u002dregister.md` \u002d\u002d The append\u002donly ledger of maintainer and operational decisions \u2014 when to write a row, when to read one, and why a settled choice should be cited rather than re\u002dargued.\n262: \u002d `device\u002dbuilds.md` \u002d\u002d Building, publishing, and safely flashing images for the handheld devices we test on, as distinct from the GENERIC_X64 VM build.\n263: \u002d `documentation\u002daccuracy.md` \u002d\u002d Keep user\u002dfacing behavior and the public rocknix.org docs in sync; don't let code and docs drift.\n264: \u002d `engineering\u002dpractices.md` \u002d\u002d General engineering practices for this codebase (high\u002dsignal; add only durable, generalizable rules).\n265: \u002d `es\u002dcode\u002dtraps.md` \u002d\u002d Sharp edges in the EmulationStation codebase, each found by debugging and each carrying its fix: button\u002dbar lifetimes, rclone's piped progress, xgettext and non\u002dASCII comments, help\u002dbar prompts, TextComponent's measuring, and where pure string code lives.\n266: \u002d `es\u002dnative\u002dui.md` \u002d\u002d EmulationStation mechanics: where the code lives, the patterns for pages, cards and background jobs, the spacing and the four surface tiers, and the code conventions. Read before building or changing an ES screen; the words it shows are in `es\u002dplayer\u002dtext.md`.\n267: \u002d `es\u002dplayer\u002dtext.md` \u002d\u002d Every word a player reads: the four tiers and the two verbs, the naming conventions, how much text a row may carry, and the outcome vocabulary every cloud run ends with. Read before writing any string an ES screen or a script shows.\n268: \u002d `es\u002dui\u002dstyle\u002dguide.md` \u002d\u002d How an EmulationStation screen looks and behaves: the seven row builders, text, confirmations, waiting, saving, reboot flags, buttons, glyphs, gating, and wizards. Read before building or changing any ES screen.\n269: \u002d `fork\u002dworkflow.md` \u002d\u002d How to develop on the fork and open clean PRs upstream without leaking personal artifacts.\n270: \u002d `instruction\u002dfiles.md` \u002d\u002d Where the canonical rules live and how they load; how to tell a stale worktree copy from the current one.\n271: \u002d `issue\u002dtracking.md` \u002d\u002d Where to file issues / tracking lists for this working copy, and how they are structured.\n272: \u002d `learning\u002dcapture.md` \u002d\u002d Capture\u002dlearning loop: when storing a memory, also consider an instruction\u002dfile abstraction and append to the dated work log.\n273: \u002d `least\u002dsurprise.md` \u002d\u002d Surprise the player as little as possible: things work as they expect and the same way every time. The tie\u002dbreaker for interface and sync decisions.\n274: \u002d `milestone\u002dphase\u002dnaming.md` \u002d\u002d Milestone bodies hold the current ordered priorities; milestone/phase prefixes identify planned work independently of issue chronology. Read before creating, moving or renaming milestone work.\n275: \u002d `player\u002dlanguage.md` \u002d\u002d Player\u002dfacing language is clear first, then as short as it can be while still clear, and sized to the space it is shown in. Applies to every label, dialog, card line and script sentence.\n276: \u002d `release\u002dcandidates.md` \u002d\u002d The standard operating procedure for every release candidate: nothing behind before the cut, a clean baseline, the candidate's build, every test, play\u002dtesting on the test device, the call, the two\u002dagent upstream audit, then the submission and builds for every test device (D\u002dWORKFLOW\u002d047).\n277: \u002d `time\u002dto\u002dplay.md` \u002d\u002d Time to play \u002d\u002d from the interface to a game's first frame, and from one game's exit to the next \u002d\u002d is a first\u002dclass goal that weighs on every cloud, sync and interface decision.\n278: \u002d `upgrade\u002dand\u002dinstall.md` \u002d\u002d Every change ships onto devices that already have state. Check the upgrade path and the clean\u002dinstall path before a build goes out.\n279: \u002d `vm\u002dfirst.md` \u002d\u002d Before any test, build proof or measurement: can this be done on the VM? Written down, answered, and only a reasoned no moves it elsewhere.\n280: \u002d `working\u002dprinciples.md` \u002d\u002d The principles this project works by, and where each one is actually enforced. A map, not a restatement \u2014 every row points at the rule that does the work.\n281: \u002d `worktrees.md` \u002d\u002d Convention for creating, placing, and managing git worktrees in this fork\n282: \n283: **Scoped** (open before touching the paths):\n284: \n285: \u002d `change\u002dlog.md` \u002d\u002d The running change log is written the day a player\u002dvisible change lands; a section is a set of claims checked against the build. (paths: `projects/ROCKNIX/packages/**`, `docs/cloud\u002dsync\u002dchangelog.md`)\n286: \u002d `council\u002dsubstrate\u002dintegrity.md` \u002d\u002d Hard, mechanically\u002denforced rules for any council or council\u002dresearch invocation. Every council member call MUST go through the Council Facilitator (`scripts/council\u002dinvoke.ts`). `runSubagent`, ad\u002dhoc `curl`/`fetch`, MCP provider tools, and bespoke scripts are FORBIDDEN as council member invocation paths. Auto\u002dloads on every council / council\u002dresearch artifact so the rule is in the orchestrator's context at the relevant moment, not buried inside a skill reference doc the orchestrator may not open. (paths: `research/council\u002dresearch/**`, `research/council\u002druns/**`, `.claude/skills/council\u002dresearch/**`, `.claude/skills/council/**`, `.claude/agents/council\u002dmember\u002d*.agent.md`, `scripts/council\u002dinvoke.ts`, `scripts/lib/council\u002dverification.ts`)\n287: \u002d `generic\u002dx64\u002dvm\u002dtesting.md` \u002d\u002d How to build\u002dtest and QA the GENERIC_X64 (x86_64) VM image locally in QEMU/KVM. (paths: `projects/ROCKNIX/devices/GENERIC_X64/**`, `projects/ROCKNIX/packages/**`, `scripts/mkimage`, `scripts/image`, `tools/vm\u002dqa`, `tools/pixelelated\u002dvm\u002dcloud\u002dboundaries`, `tools/vm\u002dserial`, `tools/vm\u002dpair`, `tools/vm\u002dstop`, `tools/vm\u002dstop\u002dtest`, `tools/vm\u002dmanager\u002dsystem\u002dcheck`, `tools/vm\u002dvisual\u002dqa`, `tools/vm\u002dwalks/**`, `tools/cloud\u002dtest\u002dbackend`, `tools/emulator\u002dexit\u002dtest`, `tools/time\u002dto\u002dplay`, `docs/vm\u002dqa\u002dlog.md`)\n288: \u002d `handheld\u002devidence.md` \u002d\u002d What a handheld keeps across a power cycle, what to capture first when one misbehaves, and how the hang\u002dto\u002dreboot path works. (paths: `projects/ROCKNIX/packages/sysutils/**`, `projects/ROCKNIX/packages/rocknix/**`, `projects/ROCKNIX/devices/*/linux/**`, `projects/ROCKNIX/devices/*/patches/linux/**`, `docs/**`)\n289: \u002d `packaging\u002dand\u002dpatches.md` \u002d\u002d Writing a package.mk and generating patches: the required fields, the templates, and how patches are produced and scoped. (paths: `packages/**`, `projects/**`)\n290: \u002d `rclone\u002dcloud\u002dsync.md` \u002d\u002d Conventions for the rclone cloud\u002dsync subsystem (save/savestate/screenshot/settings backup sync). (paths: `projects/ROCKNIX/packages/network/rclone/**`, `projects/ROCKNIX/packages/rocknix/sources/scripts/**`, `tools/cloud\u002dround\u002dtrip`, `tools/cloud\u002dtest\u002dbackend`, `tools/cloud\u002dcensus`, `tools/cloud\u002dcapture\u002dstamp\u002dtest`, `tools/last\u002dgood\u002dscripts\u002dtest`)\n291: <!\u002d\u002d rules\u002dindex:end \u002d\u002d>\n292: \n293: ## Non\u002dobvious gotchas\n294: \n295: \u002d **Check upstream early when troubleshooting.** Search relevant issue reports,\n296:   release notes and fixes, compare them with our pinned source, and record dated\n297:   links and applicability in the investigation issue. Similar symptoms are not a\n298:   confirmed match (`engineering\u002dpractices.md`, D\u002dWORKFLOW\u002d162).\n299: \n300: \u002d Script\u002donly changes (e.g. `scripts/mkimage`) do **not** trigger an image rebuild \u2014\n301:   delete `build.*/.stamps/image/build_target` first.\n302: \u002d Building from a git worktree in Docker requires mounting the main repo's `.git`:\n303:   `DOCKER_EXTRA_OPTS='\u002dv <main\u002drepo>/.git:<main\u002drepo>/.git'` \u2014 a worktree's `.git` is a\n304:   pointer file and `scripts/image` runs `git rev\u002dparse`.\n305: \u002d A network/download failure during a build often surfaces as a **misleading,\n306:   unrelated\u002dlooking build error** \u2014 check for failed downloads first.\n307: \u002d Before \"fixing\" apparently wrong code, verify design intent via `git log \u002dS`/`git blame`\n308:   and the guards around it \u2014 several dangerous\u002dlooking patterns are intentional\n309:   (`engineering\u002dpractices.md`).\n310: \u002d **Vocabulary is not decoration.** Four tiers (settings; saves; ROMs and BIOS; game\n311:   content), two verbs (*back up*, *restore*), *sync* reserved for the automatic behaviour,\n312:   \"Wi\u002dFi\" hyphenated, the serial comma, *game save* vs *save state* \u2014 `es\u002dplayer\u002dtext.md`,\n313:   D\u002dUI\u002d022. Only \"back up\" vs \"backup\" is checked mechanically (`tools/vocabulary\u002dcheck`).\n314: \u002d **Read the rules from `next`, not from a feature worktree.** A branch cut from an older\n315:   base silently lacks rule files added since:\n316:   `diff \u002dq <file> <(git \u002dC /workspace/repos/rocknix show next:<file>)`.\n317: \u002d **Headless VM QA** (no desktop on the build host): `generic\u002dx64\u002dvm run \u002d\u002dheadless\n318:   \u002d\u002ddaemonize <qcow2>`, then `tools/vm\u002dserial` for a root shell and `tools/vm\u002dvisual\u002dqa`\n319:   with `tools/vm\u002dwalks/` for the screen. SSH is disabled on a fresh image, so serial is the\n320:   way in; stop a VM by its pidfile, never by `pkill` pattern.\n321: \u002d **A worktree is removed with `tools/fork\u002dworktree remove`**, never\n322:   `git worktree remove \u002d\u002dforce`, and never the one you are standing in.\n323: \n324: ## Subsystem quick warnings (read the rule file before editing)\n325: \n326: \u002d **rclone cloud\u002dsync** (`projects/ROCKNIX/packages/network/rclone/`,\n327:   `rclone\u002dcloud\u002dsync.md`): the filter file is an *allowlist*; never put `\u002dv`/`\u002d\u002dverbose`\n328:   in `RCLONEOPTS`; `\u002d\u002ddelete\u002dexcluded` is catastrophic on a `sync`\u002dmode restore; one\n329:   active connection; a new config option goes in BOTH `cloud_sync.conf` and\n330:   `cloud_sync.conf.defaults` (`DEFAULT_` prefix); an automatic sync is bounded in seconds\n331:   while a deliberate back up may be long (D\u002dCLOUD\u002d113).\n332: \u002d **GENERIC_X64 VM QA** (`generic\u002dx64\u002dvm\u002dtesting.md`): the VM disk must be **16GB+** or\n333:   first boot breaks in a way that looks like a graphics bug; a RetroArch frame from a guest\n334:   is evidence only when its surface was the panel's size (#263); `tools/vm\u002dqa` is the\n335:   runner, `docs/vm\u002dqa\u002dlog.md` the ledger.\n336: \u002d **Physical\u002ddevice flashing** (`docs/device\u002dflashing\u002drunbook.md`, `device\u002dbuilds.md`):\n337:   identify the removable card at run time and exclude every system disk; read the raw\n338:   image back before touching its filesystem; on H700 a fresh card does not boot until the\n339:   exact device tree is activated as `/dtb.img`.\n340: \u002d **EmulationStation** (the four ES rules): comments near a `_( )` string must be ASCII or\n341:   xgettext stops the image build; `Utils::FileSystem::exists` caches a miss for the\n342:   session unless passed `false`; a button must not rebuild its own button bar.\n343: \n344: ## The checks to run before a push\n345: \n346: ```bash\n347: tools/pkgcheck <package>        # after every package.mk edit\n348: tools/rules\u002dcheck               # the instruction files: front matter, index, this file\n349: tools/register\u002dcheck            # every decision ID once, every citation names a row\n350: tools/work\u002dlog\u002dindex \u002d\u002dcheck    # the work\u002dlog index is current\n351: tools/ceremony\u002dcheck \u002d\u002dgate     # what the push guard refuses\n352: ```\n353: \n354: `.githooks/pre\u002dpush` runs the cheap ones and refuses a push of `next` when one fails; the\n355: fork CI (`.github/workflows/fork\u002dchecks.yml`) runs them on every push and daily.\n"
